::: {.doc-subject}

Check if the graph has an Eulerian circuit.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L75003-L75081){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_eulerian()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is Eulerian](../assets/graphs/is-eulerian.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: a bowtie of two triangles sharing node C; badges give each node's degree and all are even, so a closed trail can use every edge once and is_eulerian() returns True. Right: the same bowtie plus an edge between B and E, which makes B and E odd (degree 3, shown in the accent color), so is_eulerian() returns False." width="100%"}

An Eulerian circuit is a closed trail that visits every edge exactly once.

For undirected graphs, an Eulerian circuit exists if and only if:

- the graph is connected (ignoring isolated nodes)
- all vertices have even degree

For directed graphs, an Eulerian circuit exists if and only if:

- the graph is weakly connected
- every vertex has equal in-degree and out-degree
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph has an Eulerian circuit, `False` otherwise.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~eulerian_circuit`) 

:   The circuit itself.

[](`~is_semieulerian`) 

:   Check for an Eulerian path that can't return to its start.

[](`~eulerize`) 

:   Duplicate edges until the graph is Eulerian.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Every node of a square has two edges, so a walk can cross each edge once and end where it
started. A path's two ends have one edge each, so it can't:

::: {#5aeb9cdb .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

square = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D"), ("D", "A")]
)
path = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C")])

square.is_eulerian(), path.is_eulerian()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Euler's answer

`konigsberg()` is the city of Königsberg as Euler studied it in 1736, with seven bridges
joining its two islands and two river banks. Its people asked whether a walk could cross
every bridge exactly once and return home. Every land mass has an odd number of bridges,
so it can't:

::: {#972f7805 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
konigsberg = ec.konigsberg()
label = konigsberg.get_label_from_id
bridges = {
    label(n): konigsberg.get_degree(n) for n in konigsberg.get_node_ids()
}

bridges, konigsberg.is_eulerian()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
({'South Bank': 3, 'Kneiphof': 5, 'Lomse': 3, 'North Bank': 3}, False)
```
:::
:::


